    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 052048\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n148 PM PDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Shower and thunderstorm chances increase across the Mojave Desert \n  on Sunday as moisture surges northward.\n\n* Drier conditions expected for Labor Day, though monsoonal\n  moisture and warmer temperatures return midweek.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Friday.\n\nThe primary forecast concern involves a moisture surge from \nHurricane Marie, currently located several hundred miles off the \nsouthern tip of the Baja Peninsula. Southwesterly flow aloft over \nthe southwestern United States will draw moisture from the system \ninto the region, bringing PWATs into the 1.0 to 1.5 inch range. The \napproach of a 500 mb perturbation in the flow pattern supports the \ndevelopment of a band of precipitation stretching southwest to \nnortheast along the Interstate 15 corridor. However, a slight shift \nto the north or south could bring much higher precipitation amounts \nto otherwise dry areas. Widespread cloud cover and the early morning \ntiming of the mid-level perturbation support widespread stratiform \nprecipitation rather than convective thunderstorms. Flow \nperpendicular to certain mountain ranges is an excellent setup for \norographic lift and the greatest precipitation totals are expected \nover high terrain. On the Spring Mountains, there is a 70 percent \nchance or greater of one inch of precipitation over 24 hours over \n8,000 feet in elevation and a 30 percent chance of two inches of \nprecipitation. Totals in the Las Vegas Valley will be far lighter, \nbut there is a 20 to 40 percent chance of at least one quarter inch \nof rain in 24 hours. Roadway ponding and running washes are possible \nacross much of the Mojave Desert. Precipitation should end on Sunday \nnight as the disturbance moves inland.\n\nWarmer and drier conditions are expected on Labor Day as heights \naloft start to increase again. High pressure will continue to build \nand move west through the workweek. The position and strength of the \nhigh will affect how much moisture is is able to draw into the \nwestern United States, from both tropical systems and traditional \nmonsoonal sources. Temperatures are also dependent on precipitation \nand cloud cover, but rising heights aloft should generally send \nhighs back towards normal values for early September.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...South-\nsouthwest winds with gusts between 20 and 25 kts expected this \nafternoon. Expect gradually increasing cloud cover after sunset. \nClouds will increase and lower overnight with shower chances \ndeveloping as early as 12Z, but more likely after 15Z with the \npotential of seeing light to moderate rain from 15Z through 01Z. \nCIGs will generally remain around 10kft, but could drop to around \n4kft at times during the afternoon. There is a slight possibility of \nthunderstorms between 12Z-15Z, but confidence is too low to include \nin the TAF. Temperatures will remain below 100F through at least \nTuesday.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty south-\nsouthwesterly winds expected this afternoon with gust speeds between \n20 and 30 kts. The exception will be KDAG gusting from the west. \nExpect gradually increasing cloud cover this afternoon through this \nevening. Shower chances will start to increase after 12Z for the \nColorado River Valley TAF sites, KDAG, and Las Vegas Valley TAF \nsites, but as this rain band shifts north, it will mainly impact \nKDAG, KDRA, and the Las Vegas Valley TAF sites after 15Z. CIGs \ngenerally around 10kft, but may lower between 4-6kft with some \nheavier showers through the day.  \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Gorelow\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
